\begin_layout Subsection*
Why the Analytical Hierarchy Process (AHP)?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
AHP provides an axiomatic framework (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1986"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

...logic and reasoning with the aid of very small samples of data
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Ehrig2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 It is a multi-criterion decision methodology (MCDM) enjoying widespread applicability to group decision making (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1994"
literal "false"

\end_inset

),
 risk analysis (e.g.,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1987,Vaidya2006,Linkov2006"
literal "false"

\end_inset

),
 and other problems.
 AHP is often applied to situations where substantial amounts of statistically coherent measurements are not immediately available to support more-empirical approaches.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The AHP methodology begins with identifying a plausible 
\emph on
evidentiary span
\emph default
:
 The range of measurable factors —
 
\emph on
elements
\emph default
 in the vernacular of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1986,Saaty1994"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 —
 believed to differentiate within the range of scenarios under consideration.
 A 
\emph on
hierarchy model
\emph default
 represents such spans as tree graphs.
 The responsible individual decision maker or a panel of experts then performs
\emph on
 comparative judgements
\emph default
:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

...the numerical representation of a relationship between two elements that share a common parent
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in a tree graph (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1994"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 A case study herein demonstrates AHP's use to rank plausible alternatives.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Not strictly a stand-alone method,
 AHP can be fused with other approaches.
 Bayesian techniques allow for subsequent revision of the comparative judgements as stronger empirical evidence emerges (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Merrick2005,LiH2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "LiJ2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 combined back-propagation neural networks with the AHP to design shields for neutron generators.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Xiao2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 explored simulation-optimization approaches to circumvent altogether the elicitation of comparative judgements from experts.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Lukinskiy2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Sáenz-Royo(2026)"
literal "false"

\end_inset

studied methods to improve the rubustness of expert inputs into comparative judgements.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Syed 2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 describe AHP's use in eliciting prior probabilities for Bayesian reliability analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Evidentiary-strength analysis using the AHP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That comparative judgements for an AHP hierarchy model define a complete graph provides the seminal point of departure for this investigation.
 Additional opportunities arise when the resulting graph is also directed and acyclic.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1986"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 recognized this:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Partially ordered sets with a finite number of elements can be conveniently represented by a directed graph
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 Complete Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs) belong to a special class of graphs called 
\emph on
tournament graphs
\emph default
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Moon2015"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Nishizawa1999"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 obliquely considered graphical methods for AHP consistency.
 The decision-theory literature however contains scant exploration of AHP's graphical characteristics and opportunities arising therefrom.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Entropy —
 a key concept from information theory —
 provides an abstract but rigorous framework for characterizing evidentiary strength (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Cover2006"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 In general,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

the entropy of a graph is interpreted 
\emph on
as its structural information content
\emph default
 and serves as a complexity measure
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (emphasis added) (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Dehmer2011,Mowshowitz2012"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 Structural information content bears direct relevence to the present concern of evidentiary reasoning.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Brown2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 studied the particular characteristics of tournament graphs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
What questions do entropies of AHP-based graphs allow us to answer?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Endogenous to the AHP formulation itself,
 
\emph on
relative entropy
\emph default
 quantifies the marginal informational contribution by a subset of elements within the evidentiary span.
 This addresses the objective pursued by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Hué2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Graph-entropy measures however do not require extensive empirical data spanning of both positive and negative cases,
 as with Hué's approach.
 Congruent with the AHP methodology explored here,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Jiroušek2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 use entropy of
\emph on
 belief-function graphical models
\emph default
 to decompose their elements' relative evidentiary strength.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Evidentiary strength relates to the concept of 
\emph on
Value of Information
\emph default
 on which the disciplines of Information Economics (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Birchler2007"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) and Decision Analysis (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "Chapter 18"
key "Howard2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

;
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Abbas2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) are substantially based.
 The present investigation proposes an information-theoretic perspective.
 In contrast,
 information economics and decision analysis focus on utility values of information.
 Notwithstanding,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Birchler2007"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 do invoke additional concepts including information theory and probabilistic state spaces (along the lines of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "deFinetti1974"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
By what mechanism do we infer the differential information between hierarchically-adjacent AHP hierarchy models?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Relative entropy provides a well-understood approach (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Cover2006"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 Relative entropy for graphs has been well-researched (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
