\begin_layout Standard
We begin with a brief illustration of AHP methodology.
 A 
\emph on
real-world
\emph default
 case study concisely demonstrates its application to preference-ranking a set of scenarios.
 The actual ranking lies beyond our specific interest,
 but is included for completeness.
 The complete graphs resulting from the comparative judgements provide our point of departure into a novel methodology.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We then calculate the entropies for our comparative-judgement graphs associated with distinct hierarchy models.
 This extends 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Brown2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 through adoption of a Laplacian structure that includes weighted directed edges and unit-weight undirected edges.
 Our AHP-graph Laplacian fuses the structure of a conventional unit-edge-weighted directed-graph Laplacian with comparative-judgement importance intensities,
 which provide the edge weights.
 This incorporates information about degree of influence in the interconnected evidentiary framework.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 we calculate entropies and relative entropies from our AHP-based graph structure comprised of multiple,
 hierarchically coupled hierarchy models.
