\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Assigning intensities of importance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1986"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 Table 1 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1994"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 Table 1 offer a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fundamental scale
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 containing heuristics for assignment of 
\emph on
intensity-of-importance
\emph default
 values to hierarchy-model element pairs.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty2001b"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 offered a 
\emph on
psychophysics
\emph default
 justification for this scale,
 and recommended an upper bound of 
\begin_inset Formula $9$
\end_inset

 when assigning values to importance intensities.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Zeng2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 employ this scale (their 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Table 7"
target "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0308016125000316?via%3Dihub#tbl7"
literal "false"

\end_inset

),
 as do many other AHP applications appearing in the literature.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1986"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the most-rigorous description of the AHP methodology,
 contains no explicit prescription for this schema for importance-intensity assignment.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1986,Saaty2001b"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 do however speak to preferential ordering,
 transitive consistency,
 and homogeneity considerations.
 Consequently,
 alternative,
 more-rigorous importance-intensity assignments can be considered.
 Application of the personal-probability indifference principle arises as one option (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Savage1972,deFinetti1974"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Hubbard2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 —
 addressing the specific domain of cybersecurity —
 describe more-emprical approaches to 
\emph on
measurement
\emph default
.
 Application of such alternatives in lieu of Saaty's 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fundamental scale
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 deflects a common critique of AHP's logical rigor by recasting it terms of probabilism (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Pettigrew2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Constructing comparative-judgement tables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The construction of Tables 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:target-criterion-element-judgements"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:criterion-alternative-comparative-judgements"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 anticipates their use in defining directed graphs in the sequel.
 The AHP literature —
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1986,Saaty1994,Saaty2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 in particular —
 prescribes nothing about the ordering of element pairs in comparative judgement.
 Construction of Tables 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:target-criterion-element-judgements,tab:criterion-alternative-comparative-judgements"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 follows the following convention.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Populate the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

intensity of importance
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 column with the value greater than or equal to unity 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i,j}\mid(e_{i},e_{j}):\mathcal{J}_{\mathfrak{H}}\mapsto a_{i,j}\geq1$
\end_inset

 for each element pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(e_{i},e_{j})$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $(e_{i},e_{j}):\mathcal{J}_{\mathfrak{H}}\mapsto a_{i,j}<1$
\end_inset

,
 then pick 
\begin_inset Formula $(e_{j},e_{i}):\mathcal{J}_{\mathfrak{H}}\mapsto a_{j,i}\geq1$
\end_inset

 instead.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Assign to the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

reference element
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 column the corresponding most-preferred element 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{j}\mid e_{j}\succcurlyeq e_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 associated with the prefered intensity-of-importance value 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i,j}$
\end_inset

.
 This element subsequently corresponds to the head of the directed graph edge connecting the two elements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Populate the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

comparative element
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 column with the less-preferred element 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}\mid e_{i}\prec e_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 This element corresponds to the tail of the directed-graph edge.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
hspace*{
\backslash
parindent}
\end_layout

\end_inset

This convention imbues two conveniences into the procedure.
 First,
 the structure of the table endogenously contains the directive structure of the graph.
 This simplifies the algorithmic logic used to construct the resultant complete graph.
 Second,
 it inclines us to think of importance intensities in terms as personal probabilities (
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
refcite{Savage1972}{Savage}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
refcite{Savage1972}{(1972)}
\end_layout

\end_inset

;
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "deFinetti1974"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) in lieu of the less-widely-recognized 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fundamental scale
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Comparative-judgement indifference and its consequences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:target-criterion-element-judgements"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 contains unit-valued importance intensities —
 occurrences of 
\begin_inset Formula $(e_{i},e_{j}):\mathcal{J}_{\mathfrak{H}}\mapsto1$
\end_inset

 —
 raises both interest and a challenge.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
refcite{Saaty1986}{Saaty's}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
refcite{Saaty1986}{(1986)}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fundamental scale
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 explicitly allows for this.
 Four undirected edges appear in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:target-criterion-element-judgements"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
 This introduces an aspect of complexity.
 Unit-valued importance intensities imply undirected edges.
 In such cases,
 
\emph on
mixed graphs
\emph default
 occur:
 Graphs containing both directed and undirected edges.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We subsequently handle this in construction of the graph Laplacians,
 on which entropy calculations are based.
 Our Laplacians must account for both directed and undirected edges,
 as well as other-than-unit edge weights.
 Aluded to previously,
 graph-Laplacian constructions for distinct graph classes appear separately in the literature (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Wong1985"
literal "false"

\end_inset

;
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Dehmer2011"
literal "false"

\end_inset

;
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Mowshowitz2012"
literal "false"

\end_inset

;
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Brown2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

;
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Liu2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

;
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Guanlei2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 Judiciously combining graph-class-specific Laplacian structures with our mixed-graph structures represents the task of the present investigation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Transform comparative judments into 
\emph on
priority vectors
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
AHP 
\emph on
priority vectors
\emph default
 provide the key quantitative mechanism for evidentiary aggregation in MCDM applications.
 Mechanically,
 this is accomplished by constructing a 
\emph on
reciprocal matrix
\emph default
 from the comparative judgements for each hierarchy model.
 A reciprocal matrix is a Hermetian matrix with unit-valued diagonals and intensity-importance values assigned to off-diagonal elements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Construction of reciprocal matrices from comparative judgements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As the name implies,
 a reciprocal matrix is a matrix of comparative judgements within which upper-diagonal elements are reciprocals of the lower.
 Consider again a hierarchy model 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}$
\end_inset

 comprised of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 elements,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}\supseteq\left\{ e_{1},\cdots,e_{n}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 Then the corresponding reciprocal matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 becomes
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
1 & a_{1,2} & \cdots & a_{1,n}\\
a_{2,1} & 1 & \ddots & a_{2,n}\\
\vdots & \ddots & 1 & \vdots\\
a_{n,1} & a_{n,2} & \cdots & 1
\end{array}\right]\label{eq:reciprocal-matrix-general}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

subject to (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:comparative-judgement-reciprocity"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

),
 and where values of 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i,j}$
\end_inset

 are assigned according to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{J}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 as specified by (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:comparative-judgement-assignment,eq:reciprocal-matrix-diagonal-identity"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Illustratively,
 the reciprocal-matrix for criterion-to-alternative hierarchy model 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{1}$
\end_inset

 becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A_{B_{1}}=\left(\begin{array}{ccc}
1 & 3 & \nicefrac{1}{3}\\
\nicefrac{1}{3} & 1 & \nicefrac{1}{5}\\
3 & 5 & 1
\end{array}\right)\label{eq:reciprocal-matrix-B1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


