\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Priority-vector calculation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The AHP-application literature contains different approaches to translating a reciprocial matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 for a hierarchy model 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}$
\end_inset

 into a priority vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

.
 We summarize here comparisons previously described by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1984"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1990"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First,
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 is a projection onto the unit simplex 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta^{n}$
\end_inset

 where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta^{n}=\left\{ \boldsymbol{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}\left|\sum_{i}x_{i}=1\;\wedge x_{i}\geq0\:\forall i\right.\right\} \label{eq:unit-simplex-definition}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is highly significant.
 All AHP calculation results are constrained to the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[0,\,1\right]$
\end_inset

.
 This inlcudes both AHP-calculation results and standardization of inputs demonstrated in a subsequent subsection.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
Summary of priority-vector approaches.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We summarize here comparisons previously described by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1984"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 We also make passing observation of a more-empirical approach to obtaining priority vectors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subparagraph*
Principal-eigenvector method.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty2001a,Saaty2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 strongly argued for the principal eigenvector as the definitive approach to calculating priority vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

.
 Consider the eigenvector/eigenvalue decomposition
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}=\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}}\label{eq:reciprocal-matrix-eigenstructure}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

of the reciprocal matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 for a hierarchy model 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}$
\end_inset

.
 As usual,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 denotes a matrix whose dimensions correspond exactly to those of 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

,
 and whose columns are the eigenvectors thereof.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 is a diagonal matrix of the corresponding eigenvalues and 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 a vector of the diagonal elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 .
 The principal eigenvalue then appears as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lambda^{\mathcal{P}}_{\mathfrak{H}}=\left[\boldsymbol{\lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}}\right]_{\text{argmax}(\boldsymbol{\lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}})}\mbox{.}\label{eq:reciprocal-matrix-principal-eigenvector}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The priority vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 follows from the principal eigenvector as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[\boldsymbol{\omega}^{\mathcal{P}}_{\mathfrak{H}}\right]_{i}=\frac{\left[\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}\right]_{i,\text{argmax}(\boldsymbol{\lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}})}}{{\displaystyle \sum_{i}}\left[\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}\right]_{i,\text{argmax}(\boldsymbol{\lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}})}}\mbox{.}\label{eq:perron-priority-vector}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty2001a"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 grounds the rationale for this priority-vector choice in the 
\emph on
Perron-Frobenius Theorem
\emph default
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Horn2012,Hawkins2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 Priority-ordering stability by AHP proves highly sensitive to the transitive consistency of comparative judgements.
 More-strenuous than von Neumann-Morgenstern transitivity (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:vonNeumann-Morgernstern-transitivity-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

),
 AHP transitivity requires
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
a_{i,j}a_{j,k}=a_{i,k}\mbox{.}\label{eq:saaty-ahp-transitivity}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Transitive consistency applies not just to preferential ordering,
 but also of intensities of importance.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By induction,
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:saaty-ahp-transitivity"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) extends to higher orders
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
a_{1,2}a_{2,3}\cdots a_{n-1,n}=a_{1,n}\mbox{.}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 In fact,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty2001a"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 proves a limiting case
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}\frac{\boldsymbol{A}^{k}_{\mathfrak{H}}\boldsymbol{\omega}}{\boldsymbol{\omega}^{T}\boldsymbol{A}^{k}_{\mathfrak{H}}\boldsymbol{\omega}}\rightarrow\boldsymbol{\omega}^{\mathcal{P}}_{\mathfrak{H}}\label{eq:limit-perron-eigenvalue-definition}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset

given some arbitrary positive-definite 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\omega}$
\end_inset

.
 This associates 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 with the limiting case of transitive consistency specified by (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:saaty-ahp-transitivity"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subparagraph*
Geometric-mean methods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Forman1996"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Csató2019"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and others argued that arithmetic or geometric means provided suitable priority vectors in some cases.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Zeng2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 employed such a geometric-mean approach
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[\boldsymbol{\omega}^{\mathcal{G}}_{\mathfrak{H}}\right]_{i}=\frac{\left({\displaystyle \prod^{n}_{j=1}a_{i,j}}\right)^{\frac{1}{n}}}{{\displaystyle \sum^{n}_{i=1}}\left({\displaystyle \prod^{n}_{j=1}a_{i,j}}\right)^{\frac{1}{n}}}\mbox{.}\label{eq:geometric-mean-priority-vector}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1990,Saaty2001a"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 however demonstrated the accompanying risk of rank-reversal with this approach even under near-trivial conditions.
 Nonetheless,
 when a high degree of comparative-judgement transitivity (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:saaty-ahp-transitivity"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) occurs,
 results from (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:geometric-mean-priority-vector"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) converge to those produced by (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:perron-priority-vector"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

).
 The approach (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:geometric-mean-priority-vector"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) offers transparency and reproducibility advantages that many researchers find convenient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subparagraph*
Entropy-weight method.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now,
 the priority-vector methods of (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:perron-priority-vector"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) and (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:geometric-mean-priority-vector"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) are solely based reciprocal matrices constructed from subjective comparative judgements.
 Sometimes more-empirical bases for priority vectors prove useful.
 This allows for consideration of phenomenological information exogenous to comparative judgements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
Entropy-Weight Method 
\emph default
provides such an approach.
 Examples of its use appear in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
