\begin_layout Subsection*
Evaluate comparative judgements for 
\emph on
transitive
\emph default
 consistency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Glenn Shafer once opined,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

...probability is not really about numbers;
 it is about the structure of reasoning...
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (quoted in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Pearl1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 p.
 15).
 The AHP methodology contains mechanisms to identify inconsistencies and contradictions in the structure of reasoning (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty2003,Pant2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 Here,
 we consider the types of inconsistencies in comparative judgements from which concerns might arise.
 We then review 
\emph on
consistency indices
\emph default
,
 AHP's mechanism to quantify the degree of inconsistency.
 Finally,
 we consider alternative graph-theoretic approaches to mitigate inconsistencies in reasoning.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1986"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 contains an extensive set of definitions,
 axioms,
 and theorems leading to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lambda^{\mathcal{P}}_{\mathfrak{H}}\rightarrow n\mbox{,}\label{eq:reciprocal-consistency-condition}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 denotes the order of 
\emph on
perfectly consistent
\emph default
 reciprocal matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda^{\mathcal{P}}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 is defined in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:reciprocal-matrix-principal-eigenvector"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

).
 Being perfectly consistent,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 invariantly satisfies (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:saaty-ahp-transitivity"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) and (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:limit-perron-eigenvalue-definition"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Transitive consistency bears important decision-theoretic consequences:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

...when alternatives are scaled through paired comparisons,
 adding a new alternative can change the relative ranking of the old ones when the judgements are inconsistent or when several criteria are used
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1986"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
A consistency index for reciprocal matrices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A consequence of (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:reciprocal-consistency-condition"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

),
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
CI=\frac{\lambda^{\mathcal{P}}_{\mathfrak{H}}-n}{n-1}\label{eq:reciprocal-consistency-index}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

provides a commonly used 
\emph on
Consistency Index
\emph default
 for a reciprocal matrix
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Pant2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 From (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:reciprocal-consistency-condition"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

),
 one obtains 
\begin_inset Formula $CI\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

 for a perfectly consistent reciprocal matrix.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A 
\emph on
Consistency Ratio
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
CR=\frac{CI}{RI}\label{eq:reciprocal-consistency-ratio}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset

accompanies 
\begin_inset Formula $CI$
\end_inset

.
 In (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:reciprocal-consistency-ratio"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

),
 the denominator 
\begin_inset Formula $RI$
\end_inset

 results from calculating 
\begin_inset Formula $CI$
\end_inset

 for a large number of reciprocal matrices with randomly generated comparative judgements.
 Tabulated 
\begin_inset Formula $RI$
\end_inset

 values are commonly repeated.
 Reproducing the common result with atypically greater numerical precision,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Donhegan1991"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 reported 
\begin_inset Formula $RI_{3}=0.4887$
\end_inset

 for reciprocal-matrix order 
\begin_inset Formula $n=3$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $RI_{9}=1.3733$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $n=9$
\end_inset

.
 Use of these values leads to small disparities between calculations reproduced here and those reported in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Zeng2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

