noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 meet this condition.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Remediation of reciprocal-matrix transitive inconsistencies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 describes a procedure to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

...transform a positive reciprocal matrix to a near-consistent matrix.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 This involves applying perturbations to the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 following a procedure described by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Harker1987"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Performing such a procedure may lead to an epistemic conundrum:
 One applies algorithmic logic to override comparative judgements.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 elsewhere argued that 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

When we deal with tangibles,
 a pairwise comparison judgment matrix may be perfectly consistent but irrelevant and far off the mark of the true values.
 For several reasons a modicum of inconsistency may be considered as a good thing and forced consistency without knowledge of the precise values as an undesirable compulsion.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Prioritize alternatives based on comparative judgements and measurements of hierarchy-model elements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now turn our attention to prioritization of alternatives by the AHP.
 We first describe the mechanics for the general case.
 We then apply the machinery to the Titanium-alloy selection case study.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
The mechanics of prioritizing alternatives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Prioritization of alternatives by the AHP follows a linear operation.
 The following discussion employs a linear-algebra notation in lieu of commonly used nested summations.
 This offers advantages in terms of conceptual clarity.
 We develop the notation for two 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

layers
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of hierarchy models,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}_{\text{target}}\supseteq\left\{ \mathfrak{H_{1}},\cdots,\mathfrak{H}_{M}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 Induction extends the notation to more-complex hierarchical structures.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
Measurement-matrix construction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The measurement matrix contains the enumerated evidence weighed during multi-criterion decision making.
 Consider hierarchy model 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}_{n}$
\end_inset

 spanning 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 elements 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}_{n}\supseteq\left\{ e_{\mathfrak{H}_{n,1}},\cdots,e_{\mathfrak{H}_{n,N}}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 Expressed in matrix form,
 the measurement matrix for all hierarchy models within the span 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}_{\text{target}}$
\end_inset

 appears as
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{R}_{\mathfrak{H}}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\boldsymbol{r}_{\mathfrak{H}_{1}} & \boldsymbol{r}_{\mathfrak{H}_{2}} & \cdots & \boldsymbol{r}_{\mathfrak{H}_{M}}\end{array}\right]\mbox{,}\label{eq:block-enumerated-evidence}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{r}^{T}_{\mathfrak{H}_{n}}=\left[r_{\mathfrak{H}_{n,1}},\cdots,r_{\mathfrak{H}_{n,N}}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset

contains measurements for each of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ e_{\mathfrak{H}_{n,1}},\cdots,e_{\mathfrak{H}_{n,N}}\right\} $
\end_inset

 elements within the span of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}_{n}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The constituent elements for each hierarchy model 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}_{n}$
\end_inset

 must be treated as distinct entities.
 Measurements of elements 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{\mathfrak{H}_{m,m'}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{\mathfrak{H}_{n,n'}}$
\end_inset

 are plausibly —
 thought not necessarily in practice —
 independent.
 The elements 
\begin_inset Formula $\{e_{Angle},e_{Height},e_{Width}\}$
\end_inset

 in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "pemfc-hierarchy-model"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 exemplify an exception.
 Such occurrences are however rare in practice.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
Element-measurement normalization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our priority vectors reside on the unit simplex 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta^{n}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:unit-simplex-definition"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

).
 In order to constrain the prioritization values to 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[0,1\right]$
\end_inset

,
 we similarly normalize the element-measurement vectors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Normalization maps the elements of our hierarchy-model element-measurement vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{r}_{\mathfrak{H}_{n}}$
\end_inset

 to the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[0,1\right]$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{r}_{\mathfrak{H}_{n}}:\mathcal{P}\mapsto\boldsymbol{p}_{\mathfrak{H}_{n}}\mid p_{\mathfrak{H}_{n,n'}}\in\left[0,1\right]\:\forall\,n'\mbox{.}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The measurment-normalization map 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}$
\end_inset

 is defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{\mathfrak{H}_{n,n'}}=\begin{cases}
\frac{\max\left\{ \boldsymbol{r}_{\mathfrak{H}_{n}}\right\} -r_{\mathfrak{H}_{n,n'}}}{\max\left\{ \boldsymbol{r}_{\mathfrak{H}_{n}}\right\} -\min\{\boldsymbol{r}_{\mathfrak{H}_{n}}\}} & if\;e_{\mathfrak{H}_{n}}\,\text{is beneficial}\\
\frac{r_{\mathfrak{H}_{n,n'}}-\min\{\boldsymbol{r}_{\mathfrak{H}_{n}}\}}{\max\left\{ \boldsymbol{r}_{\mathfrak{H}_{n}}\right\} -\min\{\boldsymbol{r}_{\mathfrak{H}_{n}}\}} & if\;e_{\mathfrak{H}_{n}}\,\text{is deleterious}
\end{cases}\mbox{.}\label{eq:hierarchy-measurement-normalization}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Consequently,
 the normalized element-measurement matrix becomes 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{P}_{\mathfrak{H}}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\boldsymbol{p}_{\mathfrak{H}_{1}} & \boldsymbol{p}_{\mathfrak{H}_{2}} & \cdots & \boldsymbol{p}_{\mathfrak{H}_{M}}\end{array}\right]\mbox{.}\label{eq:normalized-measurement-block-matrix}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset

The 
\emph on
beneficial
\emph default
 versus 
\emph on
deleterious
\emph default
 characterizations in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:hierarchy-measurement-normalization"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) indicate whether increasing the measured value of element 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{\mathfrak{H}_{n}}$
\end_inset

 increases the or decreases,
 respectively,
 the degree to which 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}_{\text{target}}$
\end_inset

 is prefered.
 Employing a utility analogy,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{\mathfrak{H}_{n}}$
\end_inset

 is 
\emph on
beneficial
\emph default
,
 then a higher-valued measurement 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{\mathfrak{H}_{n,n'}}$
\end_inset

 indicates greater utility of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}_{\text{target}}$
\end_inset

,
 all other things being equal.
 In contrast,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{\mathfrak{H}_{n}}$
\end_inset

 is deleterious,
 then increasing 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{\mathfrak{H}_{n,n'}}$
\end_inset

 diminishes the utility of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}_{\text{target}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
Prioritization of alternatives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The priority ranking of alternatives results from a linear operation applying priority vectors to our normalized hierarchy-element measurement matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{P}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

.
 Representing the priority vectors associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \mathfrak{H_{1}},\cdots,\mathfrak{H}_{M}\right\} $
\end_inset

 in block-matrix form produces
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathfrak{H}_{1}} & \boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathfrak{H}_{2}} & \cdots & \boldsymbol{\omega}_{\mathfrak{H}_{M}}\end{array}\right]\mbox{.}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The 
\emph on
strength of preference
\emph default
 among the alternatives finally becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{p}_{\text{target}}=\left(\boldsymbol{P}_{\mathfrak{H}}\varotimes\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{\mathfrak{H}}\right)\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\text{target}}\mbox{,}\label{eq:ahp-rank-ordered-preference}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\text{target}}$
\end_inset

 denotes the priority vector associated with hierarchy model 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}_{\text{target}}$
\end_inset

 and the Kronecker product is defined as (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Graham2018"
literal "false"

\end_inset

)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\boldsymbol{A}\varotimes\boldsymbol{B}\right]_{i,j}=a_{i,j}b_{i,j}\mbox{.}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subparagraph*
What does 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{p}_{\text{target}}$
\end_inset

 represent?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The the elements of the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{p}_{\text{target}}$
\end_inset

 enumerate the 
\emph on
strength of preference
\emph default
 for each corresponding alternative within the range considered by the multi-criterion decision problem.
 Given the element-measurement normalization schema (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:hierarchy-measurement-normalization"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

),
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{\text{th}}$
\end_inset

 alternative —
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $i=\text{argmax}\left(\boldsymbol{p}_{\text{target}}\right)$
\end_inset

 —
 is the most-preferred.
 Linearly combining priority vectors obtained from comparative judgements about the degree of influence by hierarchy-model elements with measurements against each element for each alternative produces 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{p}_{\text{target}}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subparagraph*
The vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{p}_{\text{target}}$
\end_inset

 does not contain expected utilities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The principles of decision science lead us to select the alternative producing the 
\emph on
greatest expected utility
\emph default
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Kochenderfer2015"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 Chosing any other alternative constitues a departure from rationality.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Abbas2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 leveled this core criticism against decision methods based on 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

pairwise comparisons
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One might plausibly construct an AHP application so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{p}_{\text{target}}$
\end_inset

 corresponds to a utility function,
 if not an actual utility function itself.
 This must also employ a comparative-judgement intensity-of-importance assignment schema 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{J}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:comparative-judgement-assignment"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) consistent with the principles of probabilism (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Pettigrew2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1986"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 did not provide for these by default.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\boldsymbol{p}_{\text{target}}\right]_{i}\in\left[0,1\right]$
\end_inset

,
 it exhibits at least one property of a probablity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Identifying mechanics for an expected-utility-based AHP formulation lies beyond the scope of the present investigation.
 It sufficies here to reiterate this epistemic variance between AHP and mainstream decision science.
 Providing an information-theoretic characterization of the degree of influence by evidentiary elements within hierarchy models represents our goal.
\end_layout

