\begin_layout Section*
Entropy of edge-weighted,
 directed acyclic graph.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our attention now pivots to information-theoretic analysis of the hierarchical framework comprised of comparative judgements.
 This represents the essential analytical innovation.
 Within AHP applications,
 strength-of-preference based on the linear operation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:ahp-rank-ordered-preference"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) represents common practice.
 That the graph structure resulting from combining comparative judgements using hierarchy models provides greater contrast constitutes our motivating preference.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We begin with brief consideration of the comparative-judgement graphs based on Tables 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:target-criterion-element-judgements,tab:criterion-alternative-comparative-judgements"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
 Our attention then turns to Laplacian-matrix formulation for the different commparative-judgement scenarios.
 Within the context of the Titanium-alloy case study,
 Laplacians for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}_{B_{1}},\cdots,\mathfrak{H}_{B_{1}}$
\end_inset

 are straightforward,
 involving simple extensions of the approach from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Brown2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The graph associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}_{TA_{\text{opt}}}$
\end_inset

 however involves greater complexity,
 involving a mixed graph.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Following on 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Brown2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and others.
 Laplacian matrices provide the bases for graph-entropy calculations.
 Graph entropies for individual hierarchy-model comparative judgements follow straightforwardly.
 Coupling them into an overarching network involves innovative coupling using hierarchy-model priority vectors.
 That the latters' coincidence to the unit simplex proves useful,
 allowing us to treat the hierarchy-model tree graphs as probabilities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally apply relative entropy to consider the loss of information —
 increase in entropy —
 occuring when a key informational component is omitted.
 With the Titanium-alloy case study,
 the metallurgical criteria in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Hierarchy-decomposition-used"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 represent the key informational components for which we contributional characterization.
 The approach by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Jiroušek2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for belief-function graphical models informs our approach.
\end_layout

